This study tackles the persistent challenge of extracting dynamical information from noisy acoustic emission signals generated by an adhesive tape being peeled under a constant traction velocity. By applying a phase space reconstruction method, we confirm deterministic chaotic dynamics through the identification of a correlation dimension and a positive Lyapunov exponent within a specific range of traction speeds. These findings are interpreted using a model that underscores the deterministic nature of acoustic emissions by linking them to stick-slip dynamics. Complementary analyses of the Cramér-Rao lower bound and Fisher information further validate the efficacy of this dynamical tracing back to its underlying physical mechanisms. Ultimately, this approach offers a deterministic framework for organizing and understanding the complex complexity of acoustic emissions during the linear pulling of an adhesive tape. The proposed technique is broadly applicable to any system producing crackling noise due to barrier rupture under traction, including rocks during seismic events, soils under stress, foams under compression, and biological tissues such as cartilage.

